From 6f2720c59405caa8e9c6e3de639a69377cd2c64d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 10:38:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(blocks): render Bedrock state geometry and materials

Resolve custom block properties and ordered permutations after StartGame,
including per-face textures, geometry, transforms, shapes and lighting.
Reserve free note-block and redstone-wire states deterministically and deduplicate identical
visual and physical definitions. Preserve the original vanilla mapping
when a definition or a required custom texture cannot be represented.

Bedrock sends the runtime definitions after the initial pack. Keep Java
in configuration while it acknowledges a supplemental converted pack and
buffer ordered world packets with explicit count and byte bounds. Only
that pack's acknowledgement releases the scene; rejection clears custom
mappings before joining.

Saved Bedrock 1.26.51 scenes resolve all 276 CubeCraft states, all 1,795
Minehut states and all 199 Geyser states. Geyser mushroom faces use Java's corresponding vanilla
sprites when the server omits those vanilla Bedrock textures. Conversion
rejects ambiguous geometry and nonuniform scaling of rotated cubes. Legacy texture-array variation selection remains unimplemented.

Targeted tests cover permutations, transforms, face materials, lighting,
pack identity, rejection, queue bounds and one-shot completion.

Resume buffered packets with their Bedrock type after the base protocols that
already processed them. Preserve cancellation and downstream translation
without forcing either tracked connection state.

Bind the particle sprite explicitly for both full-cube and geometry block
models. The recorded Geyser scene exposed Java model warnings and missing
block particle textures despite valid face sprites.

Compose static parent-bone rotations child-to-root around their distinct
pivots and inflate cubes on both sides. Minehut's saved custom skulls need
these transforms and 1,790 distinct visuals, beyond the original 1,349
note-block carriers. Append 1,280 unoccupied, water-free wire states while
keeping note assignments stable and excluding every vanilla Bedrock
mapping. Preserve vanilla wire multipart selectors for unassigned states.
Native client adapters suppress carrier redstone particles and tint.

Exhaustive semantic tests cover all 1,296 wire states, exact custom/vanilla
partitions, compact selector conditions and ordinary note fallback.

Retain bone membership from the converter's remapped emitted group indices
when applying static block transforms. Invisible cubes emit no Java element
and must not shift later visible cubes into another bone. Regression
coverage combines invisible parent and child cubes with distinct rotated
bones, preserved face UVs, and immutable source geometry.

Decode custom light filtering from the serialized StartGame component
minecraft:block_light_filter.lightLevel. The official Bedrock 1.26.51.1
Hive capture (protocol 2193) sends a byte value of zero for cat tails,
willow leaves and transparent lamps; reading the creator JSON component
name made those blocks incorrectly default to fully opaque. Runtime
components come directly from network NBT, so retain the wire emission
field minecraft:light_emission.emission without speculative JSON aliases.
Network-NBT and permutation tests preserve transparent, partial, opaque,
emitting and omitted-component behavior.

Preserve per-face material face_dimming from the same target Hive capture.
Its serialized material instances contain 136 explicit false values.
Java 26.3 ignores the older shade flag, so use shade_direction_override
with the upward direction to remove directional attenuation while retaining
world light and ambient occlusion. Partition mixed-face elements without
changing their bounds, rotations, UVs, emission, or solid-block occlusion.

Render missing back faces for zero-thickness alpha-test block planes while
retaining the authored texture at each corner, face lighting and static
rotations. Preserve explicitly authored opposite faces, single-sided
materials, inflated planes and solid cubes. The official Bedrock 1.26.51.1
Hive recording (protocol 2193) sends red snapdragons as crossed WEST-only
planes with alpha_test and disabled face dimming. Its broad plane faces
away from the matched camera; Java culled the native-visible blossoms.
Semantic tests cover every face axis, signed UVs and quarter-turn UV
rotations, material partitions and immutable source geometry.

Recognize minecraft:geometry.full_block_v1 and preserve its bottom face UV
rotation. The pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 server (build 51061372, protocol
2193) serializes this identifier for a format 1.21.80 full-block definition.
Treating it as an external model silently discarded the custom visual and
skipped the supplemental pack. Microsoft documents the compatibility
mapping and the 180-degree bottom-face difference introduced in 1.26.0:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/blockreference/examples/blockcomponents/minecraftblock_geometry

Tests compare complete modern and legacy models after removing only the
expected bottom rotation, including simple and structured geometry fields,
collision, selection and opaque occlusion.

Transport protocol-bound collision, selection, occlusion and lighting
properties in the accepted supplemental Java resource pack. ViaProxy
clients cannot access ResourcePackStorage in the proxy process. Include
these properties in the converted-pack fingerprint so changes to physics
invalidate an otherwise identical model cache entry.

Retain the target VoxelShapes registry before StartGame with atomic, bounded
decoding. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) emits packet 337; a procedural
four-box fixture establishes X/Y/Z cell order with Z varying fastest and
low-bit-first packed occupancy. This registry describes culling, not
collision or selection. Rendering its culling rules remains incomplete.
Malformed and truncated payload tests preserve the previous registry.

Compile authored block-culling rules into individual model faces and export
transformed voxel face slices in the accepted supplemental resource pack.
Preserve bone/cube indices through invisible geometry and preserve block
identity only when a pack uses identity-based conditions, so ordinary
visual deduplication remains available. Retain opaque-neighbor controls
and default, block, permutation and culling-layer conditions.

The target 1.26.51 protocol carries voxel shapes before StartGame. Microsoft
documents culling rules and voxel face comparisons independently from
physical boxes. Numerical coverage tests retain holes and extents across
unequal grids. Quarter-turn tests align authored rules and native slices.
Vanilla partial-block shape mappings and full native comparisons still
require verification.
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/documents/voxelshapes
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/blockcullingreference/examples/blockcullingrules/block_culling

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 content diagnostics reject an
explicit default condition and accept only the three named conditions.
Represent default behavior by omitting condition in authored JSON. Keep
DEFAULT in generated metadata, where it denotes that omitted condition.
Compact adjacent grid cells before client comparisons without filling
holes. Dense-grid tests preserve missing cells.

Native local-world comparisons preserve the different-permutation cube
and retain voxel geometry beside a transparent built-in cube without
culling rules. Only authored default rules participate in voxel slices.
Do not infer participation from a built-in unit shape or conditional
rules. Preserve implicit full-cube opaque-neighbor culling independently.

The built-in full_block model has no authored bone graph. Do not invent
a block bone for user culling rules. Native transparent built-in cubes
remain outside the custom voxel comparison, while an authored custom
geometry with a default opposite rule occludes the same half-grid.
Keep both cases distinct in compiled participation metadata.

Omit faces whose cutout or blended material has no visible pixels after alpha conversion. Java 26.3 assigns zero-area UV ranges to opaque rendering, which showed dark faces for fully transparent neighbors beside the pinned native culling fixture. Keep opaque materials visible and compile authored culling participation before omitting any faces, so invisible geometry retains its physical boxes and voxel coverage. Resolve the actual sprite on copied back faces and mixed material elements.

Treat zero-volume collision and selection boxes as empty physical shapes.
Do this before rotation so an enclosing AABB cannot make an empty shape
solid, and after transformation for collapsed axes. Canonical metadata
removes empty boxes without rejecting resource-pack conversion or its
cache fingerprint. Nonfinite and inverted metadata bounds remain errors.
Regression tests cover all three empty axes, rotated models, metadata
round trips and cache identity. The reported server values still need
a capture before attributing its exact failure to this case.
